{
  "summary": "P1 credential compromise. User Aisha Khan (user_id 5, HR Generalist, aisha.khan@brightline.example, device BL-LT-0105) reports she clicked the link in a 'payroll verification' email and typed her password on the site. She did not give an exact time of the click. She did not mention approving a Duo push or opening an attachment. Matching phishing message found in her quarantine: Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain: capital I in place of l), subject 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', received 2026-10-10T21:23Z, marked phishing, not releasable. She says she clicked it, so a copy may have reached her inbox, or the same campaign may have reached her another way. Sign-in logs (14 days): 10 sign-ins, all successful, all New York US, all corporate or her usual residential IP, all during business hours with Duo push. No failed attempts, no denied MFA, no hosting-network sign-ins. Last sign-in was 2026-10-09, so nothing after the phishing email arrived. Duo is enrolled on her phone. Account is active and not blocked. Actions: password reset requested (approval id 1, pending). Temp password must go by phone after callback verification. I did not request block_sign_in because the logs show no suspicious activity. Still needed: approve the reset, confirm the click time with the user, keep watching sign-ins and Duo for unrequested pushes, review mailbox rules and forwarding for tampering, block the lookalike sender domain org-wide, and check whether other users (especially HR/payroll) got the same campaign.",
  "reason": "KB-029 says always escalate. Ticket is P1 credential compromise."
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Aisha, thank you for telling us right away. That helps a lot. We're treating your password as stolen, so we've asked for it to be reset. Once a technician approves it, they'll call you to confirm it's you and give you a temporary password over the phone. You'll then choose a new one. We'll never send it by email. Some good news: I checked your account and found no sign-ins from anyone but you. Our security team is also on this now. Until you hear from us: please don't delete that email, forward it, or reply to it. Security may want to look at it. Never approve a Duo push on your phone that you didn't start yourself. If you get one, deny it and tell us right away. If you used the same password anywhere else, change it there too. If you also opened an attachment from that email, disconnect your laptop from Wi-Fi and let us know.",
  "internal_note": "P1 credential compromise (KB-029). User 5, Aisha Khan, HR, device BL-LT-0105. She entered her password on a payroll phishing site. Click time not given. Quarantine has the matching phish: Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain), 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', received 2026-10-10 21:23Z, marked phishing, not releasable. It was not released. Sign-in logs (14 days): 10 successful sign-ins, all NY from her corporate or home IP, business hours, Duo push. No failures, no hosting networks, nothing after 2026-10-09. Duo is enrolled on her phone. Password reset requested (approval id 1, pending). Deliver the temp password by phone after callback verification. I did not request block_sign_in because I saw no suspicious activity. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation id 1) to keep watching sign-ins, check inbox rules and forwarding, block the lookalike domain org-wide, and check whether other users got this campaign.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-029",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
